# Always Read Sheet Data Live From the Google Sheet (MANDATORY)

The Google Sheet is the single source of truth. Whenever a task needs sheet
content (cell values, field labels, tab lists, existing API names, etc.), fetch
it **live from the Google Sheet via the Sheets API at that moment**. Do not rely
on any previously saved copy.

## Never read from cached / stale sources

Treat these as NOT authoritative and do not use them to answer or drive a task,
even if they exist and look complete:
- Local snapshots such as `.build/whole_sheet.json`, `.build/whole_report.json`,
  `.build/temp_updates.json`, or any earlier export.
- Data remembered from earlier in the conversation.
- Screenshots or pasted excerpts (use only as a hint; still fetch live to act).

If such a file already exists, ignore it as a data source and re-fetch. You may
overwrite it with a fresh live pull, but the live fetch must happen first.

## Required flow whenever sheet data is needed

1. Identify the spreadsheet ID and the exact tab(s) required.
2. Fetch that data live from the Google Sheet API right now (a fresh call).
3. Use only that freshly fetched data for the analysis/lookup/proposal.
4. If you just fetched moments ago in the same turn for the same range, you may
   reuse that in-turn result; otherwise fetch again.

## Interaction with other rules

- This rule governs READS only and does not authorize any write. Writing back to
  the sheet still requires the sheet-write confirmation gate.
- The scope-selection gate still applies: confirm which tab(s) to read before a
  live fetch when the target is ambiguous.
- Prefer the least data needed (specific tab/range) over pulling the whole
  workbook, but always pull it live.

## Guardrails

- If a live fetch fails (auth/permission/network), STOP and report it — do not
  silently fall back to a cached file.
- Never present cached data as if it were current. If you cannot fetch live,
  say so explicitly rather than answering from a stale snapshot.
